I'd like to make an MCP or MCP-like server for doing operations-related things.  Let's
start off with the basics: I'd like this to be written in rust.  It needs to be really
reliable, robust, and secure.  I'd like it to be able to run in multiple different ways:
via the CLI (so claude can talk to a local instance), via a secured HTTP (maybe via an
API token of some sort, definitely TLS secured in any case but probably also the ability
to use TLS certs for auth), and over SSH (which will likely be a variant of the CLI, but
rather than taking commands on the command line it'd take them via stdin, like "opsmcp
serve" and then write the command to the process stdin; maybe that's the way the local one
works as well and doesn't need any CLI arguments for the MCP interaction?).  It is going
to need some sort of permission granting system saying things like "this user is allowed
read-only access, this user is allowed read+write, where MCP commands are tagged as being
read-only or read+write, but it probably should also allow for fine-grained permissions
like "can view logs", "can modify users", "can restart services").

As far as what this service does, the goal is to allow AI agents like Claude Code to do a
variety of operations-type operations: view processes, view memory and disc statistics,
send signals to processes, start and stop and view containers and processes (like via
systemd), view logs in /var/log (and likely other configurable log locations) and
`journalctl`, view the kernel log (`dmesg`), view firewall information including rules and
counters, reset and modify rules (firewall should probably include iptables as well as
nftables), view/set sysctl values (including `/proc` and `/sys` values?), view the
filesystem, view podman + docker containers, view and modify corosync/pacemaker cluster
status, check the status of nrpe checks.  That should be a good solid list to get started.

It should also allow a the admin to define custom commands, like in our case we have a
"haproxystatus" program that allows us to tell the haproxyagent service on the system to
mark the system as up or down in haproxy, and view the current status.  Another example is
we have a script that requests a new TLS cert from our CA.  Another example is we have a
script that shows the status of our galera cluster.
